@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex introduction
@cindex overview

The @command{isomd5sum} package provides four command-line tools for
embedding and verifying integrity checksums in ISO 9660 disc images.
These checksums are stored in the application data area of the ISO 9660
Primary Volume Descriptor, a region unused by the standard specification.
This means the checksum can be verified directly from the @file{.iso}
file or from physical media without any separate checksum file.

@section Tools

@table @command
@item implantisomd5
Embeds an MD5 checksum into an ISO 9660 image.

@item checkisomd5
Reads and optionally verifies the MD5 checksum embedded by
@command{implantisomd5}.

@item implantisosha
Embeds a SHA-256 checksum into an ISO 9660 image.  This is the
preferred tool for new images because SHA-256 is stronger than MD5.

@item checkisosha
Reads and optionally verifies a SHA-256 or MD5 checksum.  Because it
understands both algorithm types it can be used as a single tool for
verifying any image produced by either implant command.
@end table

@section Checksum Storage

The checksum data is stored as a null-terminated ASCII string starting
at byte offset 883 of the ISO 9660 Primary Volume Descriptor.  The
string contains key-value pairs including the algorithm type, the hex
checksum, and the number of sectors covered.


@node implantisomd5
@chapter implantisomd5

@cindex implantisomd5

@section Synopsis

@example
implantisomd5 [@var{options}] @var{isofile}
@end example

@section Description

@command{implantisomd5} embeds an MD5 checksum into the application
data area of the ISO 9660 Primary Volume Descriptor of @var{isofile}.
The image must be a valid ISO 9660 file.

For new images, consider using @command{implantisosha} instead, which
embeds a SHA-256 checksum and is considered more secure.

@section Options

@table @option
@item --force
Overwrite any existing checksum already embedded in the image.  Without
this option, @command{implantisomd5} will refuse to modify an image that
already contains a checksum.

@item --supported-iso
Mark the image as targeted at @dfn{supported} media (e.g., a pressed
CD).  On Red Hat-based Anaconda installers, this flag bypasses the
interactive prompt that asks the user to verify the disc.
@end table

@section Exit Status

@table @asis
@item 0
The checksum was implanted successfully.
@item 1
An error occurred.  Typical causes include the file not being found,
the file not being a valid ISO 9660 image, or an existing checksum
being present without @option{--force}.
@end table


@node checkisomd5
@chapter checkisomd5

@cindex checkisomd5

@section Synopsis

@example
checkisomd5 [@var{options}] @{@var{isofile} | @var{device}@}
@end example

@section Description

@command{checkisomd5} reads the MD5 checksum embedded in @var{isofile}
(or block @var{device}) by @command{implantisomd5} and optionally
recomputes the checksum to verify the image's integrity.

The check can be aborted by pressing the @key{Esc} key.

@section Options

@table @option
@item --md5sumonly
Do not verify the image.  Instead, print human-readable information
about the embedded checksum (algorithm, value, and sectors covered)
then exit.

@item --verbose
Print a human-readable progress report as the image is read and
verified.  Without this option, no output is produced unless an error
occurs.

@item --gauge
Print a stream of numbers from 0 to 100 representing verification
progress.  This is suitable for piping into @command{dialog --gauge}
to provide a user-facing progress bar.
@end table

@section Exit Status

@table @asis
@item 0
The embedded checksum matches the image (verification passed).
@item 1
The checksum is missing, or the recomputed checksum does not match
(verification failed).
@item 2
The check was aborted by the user.
@end table


@node implantisosha
@chapter implantisosha

@cindex implantisosha

@section Synopsis

@example
implantisosha [@var{options}] @var{isofile}
@end example

@section Description

@command{implantisosha} embeds a SHA-256 checksum into the application
data area of the ISO 9660 Primary Volume Descriptor of @var{isofile}.
SHA-256 provides stronger integrity guarantees than MD5 and is the
recommended algorithm for new images.

Images produced by @command{implantisosha} can be verified by both
@command{checkisosha} and @command{checkisomd5}.

@section Options

@table @option
@item --force
Overwrite any existing checksum already embedded in the image.

@item --supported-iso
Mark the image as targeted at supported media.  On Red Hat-based
Anaconda installers this bypasses the interactive disc-check prompt.
@end table

@section Exit Status

@table @asis
@item 0
The checksum was implanted successfully.
@item 1
An error occurred.
@end table


@node checkisosha
@chapter checkisosha

@cindex checkisosha

@section Synopsis

@example
checkisosha [@var{options}] @{@var{isofile} | @var{device}@}
@end example

@section Description

@command{checkisosha} reads and optionally verifies a SHA-256 or MD5
checksum embedded in @var{isofile} (or block @var{device}).  It
supports checksums produced by both @command{implantisosha} and
@command{implantisomd5}, making it suitable as a single verification
tool for any isomd5sum-annotated image.

The check can be aborted by pressing the @key{Esc} key.

@section Options

@table @option
@item --md5sumonly
Do not verify the image.  Instead, print human-readable information
about the embedded checksum then exit.

@item --verbose
Print a human-readable progress report as the image is verified.

@item --gauge
Print a stream of numbers from 0 to 100 representing verification
progress, suitable for piping into @command{dialog --gauge}.
@end table

@section Exit Status

@table @asis
@item 0
Verification passed.
@item 1
The checksum is missing or does not match.
@item 2
The check was aborted by the user.
@end table


@node Examples
@chapter Examples

@cindex examples

@section Implant and verify with MD5

@example
# Implant an MD5 checksum
implantisomd5 myimage.iso

# Verify, printing progress
checkisomd5 --verbose myimage.iso

# Display checksum info without verifying
checkisomd5 --md5sumonly myimage.iso
@end example

@section Implant and verify with SHA-256

@example
# Implant a SHA-256 checksum (recommended for new images)
implantisosha myimage.iso

# Verify using checkisosha (supports both SHA-256 and MD5)
checkisosha --verbose myimage.iso

# Verify using checkisomd5 (also supports SHA-256 images)
checkisomd5 --verbose myimage.iso
@end example

@section Overwrite an existing checksum

@example
implantisomd5 --force myimage.iso
implantisosha --force myimage.iso
@end example

@section Verify a physical disc

@example
checkisomd5 /dev/sr0
checkisosha --verbose /dev/sr0
@end example

@section Use with a dialog progress bar

@example
checkisomd5 --gauge myimage.iso | dialog --gauge "Verifying media..." 10 70 0
@end example

@section Verify as part of a shell script

@example
if checkisomd5 myimage.iso; then
    echo "Image OK"
else
    echo "Image corrupted or checksum missing" >&2
    exit 1
fi
@end example


@node Reporting Bugs
@chapter Reporting Bugs

@cindex bugs, reporting

Please report bugs and feature requests at:

@url{https://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum/issues}

You may also send mail to the Anaconda development mailing list:
@email{anaconda-devel-list@@redhat.com}

When reporting a bug, please include:

@itemize
@item The version of isomd5sum (@code{checkisomd5 --version} or
      the package version).
@item The operating system and distribution.
@item The command line you used and the full output.
@item If applicable, information about the ISO file (how it was created,
      its size, and whether it was created with another isomd5sum version).
@end itemize


